WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
The Huffington Post published an article in 2015, titled,

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:06.280
"No, White People Will Never Understand the Black Experience."

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.760
The writer reasons that a white person,

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.260
no matter how blind to color,

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.120
can only understand so much of something they cannot viscerally experience.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.940
To her though, white people must still educate themselves

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.200
on schematic racism to help actively fight

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.540
against something they cannot truly understand.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:21.940
In the movie, "Menace II Society,"

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.300
the Hughes brothers are on this exact mission:

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:25.720
educating white people.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.060
In this video, I want to teach how movies teach prejudice

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.280
using various engaging cinematic techniques.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.680
"Menace II Society" attempts to do what the Huffington Post says is impossible.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:44.600
The movie tries to give an outsider a window into what it is like to grow up in a poor black neighborhood.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.280
And in doing so, they teach outsider to emphasize with people in that situation.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.780
The movie accomplishes this from the start in the first two moments:

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:52.700
the rioting scene

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.240
and the convenience store robbery

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:58.060
The rioting scene purposefully gives a distorted perspective of poor black people.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.620
From the opening shot, the camera looks at the carnage of the Watts Riots safely from a helicopter,

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.140
like someone would see live news footage on T.V.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.900
The camera is physically above the violence and looking down on the people rioting.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:10.340
Once we get to ride footage on the ground,

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.920
the filming is from the perspective of the rioting police.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.520
Never the black people.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.300
While the white news commentator in the background puts obvious emphasis on the cops instead of the rioters.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.980
Even the pixelation effect makes all the black people look the same,

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.400
removing their face from the story.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.100
This allows the viewer to easily generalize them as a group.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.500
Not as individuals.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.160
The Hughes brothers are cleverly capturing the black experience in a stereotypical way,

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.800
and in a way that many white people experience black communities:

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.500
from a distance and mostly with stereotypes.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:47.100
They further reinforce white stereotypes of black people by showing the main characters later robbing a convenience store,

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.000
another stereotypical black behavior.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:53.300
They established this white bias so they can spend the rest of the movie deconstructing it.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.600
After the riot footage, we go from a white commentator to a black narrator.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.100
And we no longer see the black people from a distance.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:02.000
We enter their homes and live within their personal stories.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.260
Caine: "That was the first time I had ever seen my father killing a guy,

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:07.800
but it wasn't the last.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.000
I got used to it though."

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.400
To compliment this intimacy,

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.800
the violence in this film becomes much grittier than the far, removed violence we normally see in news footage from a helicopter.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.200
The cinematography becomes clearer, colorful and up-close.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.040
This change was done intentionally by the Hughes brothers to show that violence is not like what we see in T.V.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:43.400
By first using various techniques establishing the black community as faceless, violent rioters,

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:48.600
the rapid cinematic change of the black community into up-close, relatable and vulnerable characters,

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.600
deconstructs the racial stereotypes.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.700
Start with the poor, black population as an outsider sees them.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.280
Then bring the outsider in

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.600
and challenge their initial perspective with a personal story of loss and excessive violence.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.160
Capture the racism and then deconstruct it.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:07.800
Getting white people on the inside of poor black neighborhoods is a great strategy to helping them understand the cycle of poverty.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.800
Actually, films have used bias cinematic techniques to highlight sexism as well as racism.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:26.100
This is done more subtly, but just as powerfully in "The Silence of the Lambs."

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:31.060
The main character arc in the movie is Clarice Starling overcoming the mental trauma of her childhood,

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:35.800
but there's also the backdrop of her struggling to distinguish herself in an almost all-male profession.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Men sexualize Clarice throughout the whole movie.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.400
Whether it's subtly checking her out or outright asking her on a date.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.600
We see her isolation most powerfully in subjective point-of-view shots throughout the movie.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.400
Where we see through the eyes of Clarice, all the towering male figures looking down on her.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.200
These cinematic choices put us in the perspective of the victim.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.640
Even the final scene has a man staring her down with her unable to see him,

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:59.160
echoing the men in her career, always watching her and looking down on her.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:03.900
The underlying idea is that even subjective experiences like growing up in a poor, black neighborhood,

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.060
or being a woman in a male-dominated profession,

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.000
will always be impossible to experience as an outsider.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.820
But directors use subjective filmmaking techniques to try to recreate it

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:14.400
and sometimes they do a pretty good job.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.200
Of course, they don't always get it right.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:21.900
It seems that, historically, mainstream American cinema has had trouble capturing authentic aspects of the Asian experience.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.000
Up until very recently,

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.600
Asian characters were almost always either the "sensei" who teaches using his Americanized Eastern wisdom,

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:30.000
or a racist punchline.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:35.700
Maybe the most important idea to grasp in these discussions of creating film bias is that they extend outside of fictional story-telling.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:37.200
In a famous example,

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.900
how is it possible that the police officers who physically assaulted Rodney King were acquitted?

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:42.700
There are many ways to approach this question

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:46.300
and the racial and political reasons for their acquittal are undeniable

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.600
but another reason is necessary to understanding that trial.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:54.400
And it's how the lawyers defending the Los Angeles Police Department distorted the video footage of the beatings to suit their story.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.780
The lawyers white-washed the footage

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:16.460
for their own narrative.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:20.000
This is a perfect example of racially-biased filmmaking used for evil.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:23.640
The lawyers created a narrative that white people are used to experiencing

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:27.800
and this deceit tries to justify the obviously unjust beating of Rodney King.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.600
Using biased filmmaking to create a distorted narrative is still happening by the way.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:35.300
If you ever want to test this, see how two different media outlets film the same event.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.600
If your network supports Betty DeVos, her Senate hearing uses films like this.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:40.200
If not it's filmed like this.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.000
The Huffington Post article may be right.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:44.680
White people can never truly understand the black experience,

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:49.240
and this also applies to men who never truly understand what it's like to be a woman in a male-dominant field,

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:53.300
but the closest we can get may be from a close reading of commentary on the black experience

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:54.800
like in "Menace II Society,"

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.000
or by the perspectives and camera choices like in "Silence of the Lambs."

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:03.780
Each movie uses the power of cinematography to show experiences outside of our own race or gender,

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:05.660
and that's the beauty of movies.

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:09.880
We get sucked into lives and experiences that we could never possibly experience ourselves,

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:13.300
and some directors use that ability to teach us something about the world around us.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:16.500
When these choices are used for good, we learn about the human experience.

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:18.000
But when they're used for bad,

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.500
they cloud the truth and present the world in a narrow-minded and dangerous way.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:22.740
So keep a look out.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:23.740
Thanks for watching.

